            <h2>Travel Guide: About Baltimore</h2>

            <p>Arriving in Baltimore, you step into one of the most historic cities on the American East Coast. This destination blends maritime heritage with modern urban energy, offering a diverse cultural experience on the Chesapeake Bay. </p>

            <h3>About Baltimore</h3>

            <p>Baltimore is the largest city in Maryland and a major port on the Chesapeake Bay. Known for its historic neighborhoods, world-class aquarium, and distinctive crab cuisine, it offers a mix of historical significance and contemporary culture. </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Home to the historic Inner Harbor and National Aquarium</li>
                <li>Birthplace of the Star-Spangled Banner at Fort McHenry</li>
                <li>Diverse neighborhoods from historic Fells Point to modern Harbor East</li>
                <li>Major medical and educational hub with Johns Hopkins University and Hospital</li>
            </ul>
            <p>This makes Baltimore ideal for both short visits and deeper cultural exploration. </p>

            <h3>Weather in Baltimore</h3>

            <p>The weather in Baltimore varies across seasons, and conditions can change-so planning helps. </p>

            <ul>
                <li><b>Spring (Mar-May): </b> Pleasant temperatures, ideal for outdoor activities</li>
                <li><b>Summer (Jun-Aug): </b>Hot and humid, peak tourist season with many harbor events</li>
                <li><b>Autumn (Sep-Nov): </b> Mild and comfortable, excellent for walking tours</li>
                <li><b>Winter (Dec-Feb): </b>Cold, occasional snow, least crowded season</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>What This Means for Travelers</h3>

            <p>Spring and autumn offer the most comfortable temperatures for walking. Summers are hot and require hydration and sun protection. Winters are generally mild but can have unexpected ice. Comfortable walking shoes are essential for exploring the harbor and historic neighborhoods. </p>

            <h2>Top Attractions in Baltimore</h2>

            <ul>
                <li><b>National Aquarium: </b> World-class marine exhibits located at the Inner Harbor</li>
                <li><b>Fort McHenry National Monument: </b>Historic site where the Star-Spangled Banner was written</li>
                <li><b>Baltimore Museum of Art: </b> Free admission museum with extensive collections</li>
                <li><b>Fells Point: </b> Historic waterfront neighborhood with shops, restaurants, and pubs</li>
                <li><b>Inner Harbor: </b> Central waterfront area with attractions, dining, and entertainment</li>
                <li><b>Oriole Park at Camden Yards: </b>Historic baseball stadium in downtown Baltimore</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>What This Means for Travelers</h3>

            <p>Baltimore offers a mix of history, culture, and modern entertainment. Many attractions are concentrated around the Inner Harbor area, accessible from Penn Station via Light RailLink or bus. The city is spread out, so planning your route between neighborhoods is important. Public transit serves key areas but may require transfers for some destinations. </p>

